      "message": "My idea was to add WARN_ON here as well, since S3 suspend is not even supposed to be used with pKVM enabled, so iommu_suspend() is not supposed to be called. (Although currently we don\u0027t actually prevent userspace from trying S3 suspend, since we don\u0027t disable advertising it in `/sys/power/mem_sleep`; but we could implement that later in a separate patch.)\n\nThen, looking at your patch, I though it was a good idea to not WARN_ON here, and just return an error instead, since I expected this would work \"perfectly\": iommu_suspend() fails, so the whole suspend fails and gracefully rollbacks to the original state, without trying to enter S3.\n\nBut now I tried this, and I see that is not what actually happens. When I try to enter S3 suspend on PTL:\n\n```\necho deep \u003e/sys/power/mem_sleep\necho mem \u003e/sys/power/state\n```\n\nthe result is not better than without this patch: it gets stuck for some time, and then reboots.\n\nWith some extra debugs enabled, e.g. via:\n```\necho N \u003e/sys/module/printk/parameters/console_suspend\necho 1 \u003e/sys/power/pm_debug_messages\necho 1 \u003e/sys/power/pm_print_times\necho 0 \u003e/sys/power/pm_async\n```\n\nI see this is what happens, for example:\n\n```\n...\n\u003c7\u003e[  146.628502][ T3281] PM: noirq suspend of devices complete after 952.167 msecs\n\u003c6\u003e[  146.628509][ T3281] ACPI: PM: Preparing to enter system sleep state S3\n\u003c6\u003e[  146.631679][ T3281] ACPI: EC: event blocked\n\u003c6\u003e[  146.631722][ T3281] ACPI: EC: EC stopped\n\u003c6\u003e[  146.631766][ T3281] ACPI: PM: Saving platform NVS memory\n\u003c6\u003e[  146.631958][ T3281] Disabling non-boot CPUs ...\n\u003c6\u003e[  146.634276][ T3281] smpboot: CPU 7 is now offline\n\u003c6\u003e[  146.637976][ T3281] smpboot: CPU 6 is now offline\n\u003c6\u003e[  146.641123][ T3281] smpboot: CPU 5 is now offline\n\u003c6\u003e[  146.644243][ T3281] smpboot: CPU 4 is now offline\n\u003c6\u003e[  146.647003][ T3281