      "message": "\u003e I still think we should just WARN_ON here. iommu_suspend() doesn\u0027t seem to be the right place for printing logs like \"pKVM does not support S3 suspend\", since S3 is not about IOMMU, and iommu_suspend() is just one of many S3-specific suspend callbacks in the kernel. \n\nI agree, The message seems a bit off - probably \"IOMMU suspend callback with pKVM enabled\" or something? My idea of WARN_ON usage is if a code path that should not happen - like iommu_resume() being called if iommu_suspend() fails. But I do not have a strong opinion and shall use WARN_ON() \n\n\u003e (Also, ratelimiting is not quite needed in this case?)\n\nSince this is user initiated which could be a loop as well(like a stress suspend/resume test), I thought rate limiting would be better.\n\n\u003e And again, per the above, if we reached iommu_suspend() while pKVM is enabled, we are already in a pretty bad unrecoverable state, so a noisy WARN_ON seems more appropriate?\n\nI am fine with this. Will change it.",
